# UGV02 chassis MCU: a CH343/CH9102 USB-serial bridge (1a86:55d3) that presents
# itself as CDC-ACM, so the kernel binds cdc_acm and it enumerates as an
# arbitrary ttyACM* depending on what else was plugged in first - exactly the
# problem /dev/ldlidar solves for the lidar. einride_mini_truck_hardware's
# config/hardware.yaml names the port as /dev/ugv02, so this rule is what
# makes that name exist.
#
# Matched by vendor and product rather than by ATTRS{serial}, for the same
# reason 99-einride-ldlidar.rules matches the CP210x that way: the serial is
# per-board, so a rule carrying one would have to be edited for every robot.
# The cost is that a second 1a86:55d3 bridge on the same machine would race for
# the symlink. If that ever happens, add ATTRS{serial}=="..." to this line and
# accept that the file is then per-robot.
#
# MODE and GROUP restate what Ubuntu's own 50-udev-default.rules already gives a
# ttyACM*, so this rule grants no access the chassis did not already have; the
# stable name is the whole point of it. Being in dialout is still required, and
# provision.sh puts the service account there.
KERNEL=="ttyACM*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="55d3", \
    MODE:="0660", GROUP:="dialout", SYMLINK+="ugv02"
